{% set local_problem %}
<p>Arab homes face a challenge flat-land properties don't: gravity-concentrated water flow. When you're on Brindlee Mountain and it rains, water doesn't just fall on your roof — it flows faster down steep pitches and hits gutters with more force. Your gutters need to handle more velocity than the square footage of your roof might suggest.</p>

<p>Many older <a href="/recent-projects/">Arab homes</a> still have original sectional gutters well past their effective lifespan. The seams have separated, debris accumulates at every joint, and water cascades over the sides during moderate rain. On sloped terrain, that overflow heads straight toward your foundation at increased velocity — roughly 1.5x faster on a 10% grade.</p>

<p>Even newer Arab homes often have undersized gutters installed by builders focused on cost rather than performance. When those 5-inch sectional gutters with 2x3 downspouts meet a mountain thunderstorm, they simply can't keep up. Water backs up, overflows, and the resulting erosion and foundation damage costs far more than proper gutters would have.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> On Arab's terrain, don't compromise on gutter capacity. The cost difference between 5-inch and 6-inch gutters is maybe 15-20% — but the capacity difference is 40%. For mountain homes where water moves faster, that extra capacity is worth it.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Seamless gutters are continuous gutters with no seams except at corners and downspout connections. Unlike sectional gutters made of pre-cut 10-foot sections joined together, seamless gutters are fabricated on-site from one continuous roll of aluminum coil — cut to your home's exact run lengths.</p>

<p><strong>What we install:</strong> 6-inch K-style gutters in .032 gauge aluminum (about 20% thicker than the .027 on builder-grade systems). Hidden hangers at 24-inch intervals. 3x4 downspouts every 35-40 linear feet. Everything measured and fabricated in your driveway.</p>

<p><strong>Why those specs matter for Arab:</strong> Steeper roof pitches mean water hits gutters faster. Thicker aluminum withstands the impact better. Tighter hanger spacing (24 inches vs 32-inch code minimum) prevents sagging when debris accumulates. Larger 3x4 downspouts handle the concentrated flow without backing up.</p>

<p><strong>The honest bit:</strong> "Seamless" is slightly marketing. You still have seams at inside corners, outside corners, and downspout drops. What you eliminate is the seam every 10 feet in the middle of runs — and those mid-run seams are what fail first, especially under the stress of mountain rainfall patterns.</p>
{% endset %}

